  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Epilepsy of unknown type, recurrent generalized tonic-clonic seizures (G40.909)</content>. Neurology confirms epilepsy: many unprovoked seizures over years. With no clear onset and a normal EEG and MRI, the seizures are classified as generalized tonic-clonic seizures of unknown onset and the epilepsy as of unknown type, with unknown cause. Treatment is clearly indicated. After discussion of valproate, levetiracetam, and lamotrigine, he chose lamotrigine, which suits an epilepsy of unknown type, does not lower mood, and does not affect bone metabolism. [S]<list><item>Start lamotrigine today: 25 mg daily for two weeks, 50 mg daily for two weeks, 50 mg twice daily for one week, 75 mg twice daily for one week, then 100 mg twice daily.</item><item>Stop it and call the same day for any rash, mouth sores, fever, or swollen glands.</item><item>Sleep-deprived EEG in three weeks.</item><item>Keep a seizure diary on his phone, with sleep, missed doses, and possible triggers.</item><item>Seizure action plan written today and shared with two friends; SUDEP discussed with neurology today.</item><item>No driving until seizure-free and cleared by his neurologist; showers rather than baths; no swimming alone.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gambling disorder (F63.0)</content>. Abstinent for four weeks with self-exclusion, a bank block, a co-signer, and weekly therapy in place. Safety reviewed: no thoughts of suicide. [S]<list><item>Continue weekly cognitive behavioral therapy.</item><item>Gamblers Anonymous offered; he will try a meeting.</item><item>Appointment with a free nonprofit debt counselor.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Seizure frequency, seizure diary, and seizure description</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One unwitnessed generalized seizure three weeks ago after a night without sleep; diary started today</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>EEG (routine, then sleep-deprived if normal)</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Routine EEG two weeks ago normal; a sleep-deprived EEG is ordered, because a single routine EEG often misses epileptiform discharges</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>MRI of the brain, epilepsy protocol</td><td>At diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Normal two weeks ago, with no hippocampal sclerosis, cortical malformation, or other lesion</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Seizure and epilepsy classification (ILAE)</td><td>At diagnosis, and again if new seizure types or results appear</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Generalized tonic-clonic seizures of unknown onset; epilepsy of unknown type; unknown cause</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Blood count and liver tests</td><td>Before starting; not routinely repeated on lamotrigine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Normal blood count and liver tests four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antiseizure medicine adverse effects (dizziness, double vision, unsteadiness, sleep, mood)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Lamotrigine starts today; reviewed from the next visit</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Rash and mouth-sore check (lamotrigine)</td><td>Every contact during titration and the first two months, then with symptoms</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No rash at baseline; warning signs taught</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Drug-interaction review</td><td>With every new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No other medicines; no interactions with lamotrigine</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Trigger review: sleep loss, alcohol, overwork, missed doses</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Writing through the night preceded the last seizure; plan to stop writing after midnight</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Seizure safety and driving counseling</td><td>At diagnosis and every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Not driving; bathing, swimming, heights, and cooking precautions reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>SUDEP discussion</td><td>At diagnosis and when seizure control changes</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Discussed with neurology: the risk is mainly from uncontrolled generalized tonic-clonic seizures, especially at night when alone, and falls with good control</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Written seizure action plan for the people around him</td><td>At diagnosis, then yearly</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Written today and shared with two friends</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Rescue medicine for prolonged seizures or clusters</td><td>If seizures are prolonged or come in clusters</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Seizures are single and brief, with no clusters or prolonged seizures</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Bone health: vitamin D, and DXA on enzyme-inducing medicines</td><td>At the start of long-term treatment</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Vitamin D 32 ng/mL four weeks ago; lamotrigine is not an enzyme inducer, so no DXA scan</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Depression screen (PHQ-9)</td><td>At intake, then every two to four months while symptoms are present</td><td>Up to date</td><td>PHQ-9 8 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Anxiety screen (GAD-7)</td><td>At intake and with any change in mood</td><td>Up to date</td><td>GAD-7 7 four weeks ago</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Suicide-risk review and safety plan, with the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline</td><td>Every visit (gambling disorder and antiseizure-medicine labeling)</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No thoughts of suicide; the 988 Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline and the problem-gambling helpline are in his phone</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Gambling since the last visit: days, money lost, and urges (G-SAS for the past week)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No gambling since the first visit; strong evening urges</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Cognitive behavioral therapy for gambling disorder</td><td>Weekly sessions for at least 8 to 12 weeks, then as needed</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Started; two sessions so far</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Self-exclusion from casinos and online gambling</td><td>At the start of treatment; renew and extend after any lapse</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Casinos in the state and licensed online sites</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Financial safeguards (bank gambling block, co-signer for large withdrawals, limited cash)</td><td>At the start of treatment</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Bank gambling block on his cards; a friend co-signs any withdrawal over a set amount</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Peer support (Gamblers Anonymous)</td><td>Offered at the start of treatment and after any lapse</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Offered; he will try a meeting</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Debt counseling</td><td>Once, early in treatment</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Appointment booked with a free nonprofit debt counselor</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicine for gambling disorder (opioid antagonist)</td><td>Considered if psychological treatment alone is not enough</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Psychological treatment has only just started</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Neurology consultation</td><td>Once at diagnosis, then as advised</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Seen today</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Reach the lamotrigine maintenance dose without rash by the two-month visit. In progress; target 2026-05-05 [S]</item><item>No seizures for six months after reaching the maintenance dose. In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item><item>Casino and online self-exclusion and a bank gambling block in place by the four-week visit. Outcome by 2026-03-03: Self-exclusion from casinos in the state and from licensed online sites, and a gambling block on his bank cards, in place [S]</item><item>No gambling through the eight-month visit. In progress; target 2026-10-06 [S]</item><item>Written seizure action plan, safety counseling, and a SUDEP discussion completed by the four-week visit. Outcome by 2026-03-03: Plan written and shared with two friends; SUDEP discussed with the neurologist [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults: Report of the Guideline Development Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology and the American Epilepsy Society (2015); Practice guideline update summary: Efficacy and tolerability of the new antiepileptic drugs I: Treatment of new-onset epilepsy (2018); Operational classification of seizure types by the International League Against Epilepsy: Position Paper of the ILAE Commission for Classification and Terminology (2017); ILAE classification of the epilepsies: Position paper of the ILAE Commission for Classification and Terminology (2017); Practice guideline summary: Sudden unexpected death in epilepsy incidence rates and risk factors (2017); Psychological therapies for pathological and problem gambling (2012); VA/DoD Clinical Practice Guideline for Assessment and Management of Patients at Risk for Suicide (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Sleep-deprived EEG in three weeks. Two-month review once the maintenance dose is reached. Call the same day for any rash or a seizure. Next scheduled visit 2026-05-05: two-month review: lamotrigine at maintenance dose, rash and mood check, and gambling treatment progress. [S]</paragraph></text>

